1
00:00:01,914 --> 00:00:03,525
[instrumental music]

2
00:00:07,616 --> 00:00:09,531
[indistinct chattering]

3
00:00:12,273 --> 00:00:14,840
What brings us to
this beautiful place, Lisbon?

4
00:00:14,927 --> 00:00:16,625
A missing person.

5
00:00:16,712 --> 00:00:19,758
Jay Banner. He's 26 years old.
He's a local boy.

6
00:00:19,845 --> 00:00:22,587
He works at some sort
of marine salvage place.

7
00:00:22,674 --> 00:00:25,721
He went surfing yesterday,
and he never came home.

8
00:00:25,808 --> 00:00:28,724
Oh, look at the crabs.
You see the little crab?

9
00:00:30,900 --> 00:00:32,249
Oh, look at the little crabby.

10
00:00:34,338 --> 00:00:36,949
Excuse me.
I'm Agent Lisbon with the CBI.

11
00:00:37,037 --> 00:00:40,214
This is Consultant Patrick Jane.

12
00:00:40,301 --> 00:00:43,826
- Are you, uh, Chief Anson?
- That's right.

13
00:00:43,913 --> 00:00:47,134
These are Jay's roommates,
Tark Mitchum, Vicki Lang.

14
00:00:47,221 --> 00:00:48,352
Welcome.

15
00:00:48,439 --> 00:00:50,833
Oh, come on.
We're not welcome.

16
00:00:50,920 --> 00:00:53,488
You resent us being here
on your turf.

17
00:00:53,575 --> 00:00:55,838
Let me rephrase that,
your, uh..

18
00:00:55,925 --> 00:00:58,493
...your sand,
your sandy coastline.

19
00:00:58,580 --> 00:01:00,234
Not at all.

20
00:01:00,321 --> 00:01:04,020
It's just that Jay's an adult.
Barely been gone a day.

21
00:01:04,107 --> 00:01:08,024
This just seems to me to be
a complete waste of time.

22
00:01:08,111 --> 00:01:09,678
Is this where
he was last seen?

23
00:01:09,765 --> 00:01:12,550
Yeah, him and Jeeter.
That's our other roommate.

24
00:01:12,637 --> 00:01:14,770
They went out yesterday
for dawn patrol.

25
00:01:14,857 --> 00:01:16,337
That's an early morning
surf session.

26
00:01:16,424 --> 00:01:17,686
I know.

27
00:01:17,773 --> 00:01:19,514
And Jay didn't come home
all night.

28
00:01:19,601 --> 00:01:22,908
I found his board right here,
which means he didn't drown.

29
00:01:22,995 --> 00:01:24,954
I mean, he wouldn't drown.
He was a fish.

30
00:01:25,041 --> 00:01:26,564
So I thought,
what if he's been kidnapped?

31
00:01:26,651 --> 00:01:28,305
So, I called the cops.

32
00:01:28,392 --> 00:01:30,655
You called your father.
The congressman?

33
00:01:30,742 --> 00:01:32,918
I wanted the big guns, chief.

34
00:01:33,005 --> 00:01:34,572
That board's a custom.

35
00:01:34,659 --> 00:01:35,921
So either Jay was very rich

36
00:01:36,008 --> 00:01:37,793
or very serious
about his surfing.

37
00:01:37,880 --> 00:01:39,403
He's serious, dude.
He's trying to go pro.

38
00:01:39,490 --> 00:01:42,102
All he needs is that
one big endorsement.

39
00:01:42,189 --> 00:01:43,712
Okay. Well, don't mind me.

40
00:01:43,799 --> 00:01:47,281
I'm gonna take a little stroll,
soak up the atmosphere.

41
00:01:47,368 --> 00:01:49,631
'Oh! So gorgeous.'

42
00:01:49,718 --> 00:01:52,547
Did he have any troubles?
Drug problems?

43
00:01:52,634 --> 00:01:54,549
Bad relationships?

44
00:01:54,636 --> 00:01:56,116
No, I mean..

45
00:01:58,074 --> 00:01:59,858
No.

46
00:01:59,945 --> 00:02:01,295
- Shut up, Tark.
- Wh..

47
00:02:03,732 --> 00:02:06,909
Um, me and Jay used to hook up,
not for a long time.

48
00:02:06,996 --> 00:02:08,302
When did you break up?

49
00:02:08,389 --> 00:02:10,086
We weren't dating.
It was casual.

50
00:02:10,173 --> 00:02:11,870
- That's how Jay wanted it.
- 'Oh!'

51
00:02:15,613 --> 00:02:16,788
What is he doing?

52
00:02:18,529 --> 00:02:20,270
Big gun, huh?

53
00:02:20,357 --> 00:02:21,532
Lisbon!

54
00:02:25,275 --> 00:02:26,407
Flies and crabs!

55
00:02:27,669 --> 00:02:29,105
Excuse me?

56
00:02:29,192 --> 00:02:31,455
Crabs are scavengers,
so I followed one.

57
00:02:31,542 --> 00:02:34,154
Led me to this swarm of flies,
and look.

58
00:02:35,546 --> 00:02:36,504
[flies buzzing]

59
00:02:36,591 --> 00:02:37,853
'I found Jay Banner.'

60
00:02:41,596 --> 00:02:43,293
[flies buzzing]

61
00:02:48,124 --> 00:02:49,865
[Lisbon]
The victim worked
for this outfit here.

62
00:02:49,952 --> 00:02:51,171
Santa Marta Salvage.

63
00:02:52,955 --> 00:02:53,999
Excuse me?

64
00:02:56,480 --> 00:02:59,396
Excuse me, ma'am. Hi.
Are you Greta Marshall?

65
00:02:59,483 --> 00:03:01,050
Uh, yeah. Sure am.

66
00:03:01,137 --> 00:03:02,834
I'm Agent Lisbon
with the CBI.

67
00:03:02,921 --> 00:03:04,488
We just wanted to ask you
a few questions

68
00:03:04,575 --> 00:03:06,447
about your employee,
Jay Banner.

69
00:03:06,534 --> 00:03:08,536
Jay.

70
00:03:08,623 --> 00:03:11,016
Oh, that was such
a terrible thing, awful.

71
00:03:11,103 --> 00:03:12,844
News travels fast, huh?

72
00:03:12,931 --> 00:03:14,237
Small town, big ears.

73
00:03:16,283 --> 00:03:19,895
You should talk to my husband
too. Um, we co-own the company.

74
00:03:19,982 --> 00:03:21,375
Hey, Greg! Honey!

75
00:03:24,900 --> 00:03:26,380
What are you doing?

76
00:03:26,467 --> 00:03:27,946
I'm just checking
the nav equipment.

77
00:03:28,033 --> 00:03:30,601
Oh, checking the nav equipment.

78
00:03:30,688 --> 00:03:32,386
We had a big storm
come in here last month.

79
00:03:32,473 --> 00:03:34,170
Stirred everything up.

80
00:03:34,257 --> 00:03:36,520
She hit an uncharted sandbar
and, well..

81
00:03:36,607 --> 00:03:38,435
...knocked the sonar around.

82
00:03:38,522 --> 00:03:39,480
Ouch!

83
00:03:40,829 --> 00:03:41,917
Hey, what's up?

84
00:03:43,223 --> 00:03:44,789
Greg?

85
00:03:44,876 --> 00:03:47,139
Oh, my God.

86
00:03:47,227 --> 00:03:48,445
'What are you doing here?'

87
00:03:48,532 --> 00:03:50,360
You live here?
In California?

88
00:03:50,447 --> 00:03:52,319
Yeah, 14 years now.

89
00:03:52,406 --> 00:03:54,625
Uh, Greta's from Santa Marta.

90
00:03:54,712 --> 00:03:56,758
My-my wife, I, um..

91
00:03:56,845 --> 00:03:59,761
Honey, this is,
uh, Teresa Lisbon.

92
00:04:01,719 --> 00:04:03,068
This is Teresa Lisbon?

93
00:04:04,505 --> 00:04:05,854
TheTeresa Lisbon?

94
00:04:08,770 --> 00:04:09,684
The one and only.

95
00:04:11,816 --> 00:04:16,473
Uh, Greg and I used to date when
we were kids back in Chicago.

96
00:04:16,560 --> 00:04:18,214
Yeah, we were engaged,
in fact.

97
00:04:20,521 --> 00:04:23,872
Okay. Uh, let me guess.
You broke it off?

98
00:04:26,962 --> 00:04:29,704
I was way too young.

99
00:04:29,791 --> 00:04:31,488
Yeah, well, of course.
Did she break your heart?

100
00:04:31,575 --> 00:04:32,837
[chuckles]
I wouldn't say that.

101
00:04:32,924 --> 00:04:35,536
- No.
- Yeah, you did.

102
00:04:35,623 --> 00:04:38,147
'Cause he was still talking
about you when we met.

103
00:04:38,234 --> 00:04:40,280
[laughs]
Heartbreaker.

104
00:04:43,500 --> 00:04:46,155
We just w-wanted to ask you
some questions about Jay.

105
00:04:46,242 --> 00:04:47,896
Is there somewhere
more private we can talk?

106
00:04:47,983 --> 00:04:49,680
Sure, yeah,
we got offices up there.

107
00:04:52,553 --> 00:04:54,381
Do you have any tea?
'Cause this could take a while.

108
00:04:54,468 --> 00:04:55,599
Mm-hmm.

109
00:05:00,343 --> 00:05:02,737
[theme music]

110
00:05:12,573 --> 00:05:14,052
[Lisbon]
'How long did Jay work here?'

111
00:05:14,139 --> 00:05:16,011
Well, about a year.

112
00:05:16,098 --> 00:05:18,448
Until last week.
He-he quit.

113
00:05:18,535 --> 00:05:20,537
[Lisbon]
'Oh!'

114
00:05:20,624 --> 00:05:23,497
[Greg]
'Yeah, lately he had
something on his mind.'

115
00:05:23,584 --> 00:05:25,629
I mean, he was only doing this
for side money, anyways

116
00:05:25,716 --> 00:05:27,022
while he did his surf thing.

117
00:05:27,109 --> 00:05:28,197
Mom.

118
00:05:28,284 --> 00:05:29,938
[Greg]
Hey, baby.

119
00:05:30,025 --> 00:05:31,722
I need money for the movie.

120
00:05:31,809 --> 00:05:33,898
Excuse me?

121
00:05:33,985 --> 00:05:36,510
May I please have money
for the movie?

122
00:05:36,597 --> 00:05:38,468
- And popcorn.
- Here.

123
00:05:38,555 --> 00:05:42,559
Hi. I'm Patrick Jane.
That's, uh, the Teresa Lisbon.

124
00:05:42,646 --> 00:05:44,344
Hi. I'm Lauren Tayback.

125
00:05:44,431 --> 00:05:46,607
Hi, Lauren.

126
00:05:46,694 --> 00:05:48,260
Okay, go.
Have fun, babe. Bye.

127
00:05:48,348 --> 00:05:49,566
- Okay.
- Bye.

128
00:05:49,653 --> 00:05:51,394
Nice to meet you,
Lauren Tayback.

129
00:05:51,481 --> 00:05:52,743
Wow. How many do you have?

130
00:05:52,830 --> 00:05:55,093
Uh, we got Lauren,
Maddy, and Sally.

131
00:05:55,180 --> 00:05:57,444
[whistles]
Wow.

132
00:05:57,531 --> 00:05:58,706
Breeder.

133
00:06:01,099 --> 00:06:03,493
Yeah.

134
00:06:03,580 --> 00:06:05,974
Uh, you didn't, uh,
like Jay much, did you?

135
00:06:06,061 --> 00:06:08,672
What? No, he was
a great diver, man.

136
00:06:08,759 --> 00:06:10,282
Yes, but you didn't
like him. Why?

137
00:06:11,936 --> 00:06:14,591
It's...I have three daughters,
alright?

138
00:06:14,678 --> 00:06:16,158
And I wouldn't want any of them

139
00:06:16,245 --> 00:06:17,681
being treated the way
Jay treated women.

140
00:06:17,768 --> 00:06:18,813
How is that?

141
00:06:20,205 --> 00:06:21,293
Use 'em and toss 'em.

142
00:06:23,034 --> 00:06:24,645
Uh, why did Jay quit?

143
00:06:24,732 --> 00:06:27,648
[chuckles]
Oh, why do people quit?

144
00:06:27,735 --> 00:06:29,301
Hmm.

145
00:06:29,389 --> 00:06:30,694
You know,
they just quit.

146
00:06:33,393 --> 00:06:34,829
- Excuse me.
- See you, babe.

147
00:06:38,441 --> 00:06:40,269
She's nice.

148
00:06:40,356 --> 00:06:42,227
[Van Pelt]
Uh, Rigsby's
taking a personal day.

149
00:06:42,314 --> 00:06:43,968
The baby's sick,
and Sarah has court.

150
00:06:46,144 --> 00:06:47,711
He's really embracing
the daddy thing.

151
00:06:47,798 --> 00:06:48,799
Yeah.

152
00:06:50,279 --> 00:06:51,672
[knocking on door]

153
00:06:57,242 --> 00:06:58,679
You guys the cops?

154
00:06:58,766 --> 00:07:00,420
Yeah.

155
00:07:00,507 --> 00:07:03,553
CBI. Are you Jay Banner's
other roommate?

156
00:07:03,640 --> 00:07:07,035
No. Uh, I'm the landlord.
Lance Gladstone.

157
00:07:07,122 --> 00:07:09,037
I, uh, own this place.

158
00:07:09,124 --> 00:07:11,866
I live in the, uh, back house.

159
00:07:11,953 --> 00:07:14,695
I was just gonna do
some work on these steps.

160
00:07:14,782 --> 00:07:16,784
Keep my mind off Jay,
you know?

161
00:07:16,871 --> 00:07:18,960
You have keys to let us in?

162
00:07:19,047 --> 00:07:20,309
Sure.

163
00:07:20,396 --> 00:07:21,876
[keys jangling]

164
00:07:28,578 --> 00:07:29,797
What?

165
00:07:32,277 --> 00:07:34,671
[Van Pelt]
'Jeeter, how long
were you and Jay roommates?'

166
00:07:34,758 --> 00:07:37,326
Three years. Jay and me.

167
00:07:39,241 --> 00:07:42,070
We were both trying
to qualify for the pro tour.

168
00:07:42,157 --> 00:07:44,159
And Jay was really close
to inking a deal

169
00:07:44,246 --> 00:07:45,900
with Oakley, you know?

170
00:07:45,987 --> 00:07:47,902
Mind if I open a window?

171
00:07:47,989 --> 00:07:50,426
Please. This smoke
plays hell with my asthma.

172
00:07:50,513 --> 00:07:52,863
I have a prescription.

173
00:07:52,950 --> 00:07:54,952
I have anxiety.

174
00:07:55,039 --> 00:07:56,867
Especially now.

175
00:07:56,954 --> 00:07:59,740
Jay was my best friend.

176
00:07:59,827 --> 00:08:02,003
And you two went surfing
together yesterday morning?

177
00:08:02,090 --> 00:08:04,527
For a while, and then
it got kind of blown out.

178
00:08:05,963 --> 00:08:07,443
So I went to work.

179
00:08:07,530 --> 00:08:08,749
I left at 7:00.

180
00:08:08,836 --> 00:08:10,315
Jay was alive
when you left?

181
00:08:10,402 --> 00:08:12,927
Yes. God, yes.

182
00:08:13,014 --> 00:08:16,583
He was standing there
on the beach.

183
00:08:16,670 --> 00:08:19,847
Coroner's preliminary report
says Jay died yesterday morning

184
00:08:19,934 --> 00:08:21,544
around the time
you were with him.

185
00:08:21,631 --> 00:08:23,241
Did you see anything unusual?

186
00:08:23,328 --> 00:08:24,765
Maybe someone else on the beach?

187
00:08:24,852 --> 00:08:26,593
No. No one on the beach.

188
00:08:28,551 --> 00:08:31,075
But there was a boat coming in.

189
00:08:31,162 --> 00:08:32,686
Coming in?
That's weird for that hour.

190
00:08:32,773 --> 00:08:33,904
Most boats are going out.

191
00:08:33,991 --> 00:08:37,081
- You see the name of the boat?
- No.

192
00:08:37,168 --> 00:08:39,040
Couldn't see
with the marine layer.

193
00:08:39,127 --> 00:08:41,433
Running lights weren't on.

194
00:08:41,521 --> 00:08:42,739
Interesting.

195
00:08:42,826 --> 00:08:44,611
Somebody didn't want to be seen.

196
00:08:44,698 --> 00:08:48,179
Marine salvage.
What is that, exactly?

197
00:08:48,266 --> 00:08:51,095
We do lots of different stuff,
like water rescue

198
00:08:51,182 --> 00:08:53,794
harbor clean-up,
wreck diving ops.

199
00:08:53,881 --> 00:08:55,883
Wreck diving ops? That sounds
like treasure hunting.

200
00:08:55,970 --> 00:08:57,711
[chuckles]
No, not so much.

201
00:08:57,798 --> 00:08:59,887
We got a lot of old ships
on the ocean floor around here.

202
00:08:59,974 --> 00:09:01,802
'That's pictures of some of them
on the wall there.'

203
00:09:01,889 --> 00:09:03,804
But they've all been
picked over for decades now.

204
00:09:03,891 --> 00:09:05,545
There's nothing down there
but some..

205
00:09:05,632 --> 00:09:06,807
...cool wrecks
to dive around.

206
00:09:06,894 --> 00:09:08,243
Uh, the Brother Jonathan

207
00:09:08,330 --> 00:09:10,375
uh, the Golden State,
and the Fremont..

208
00:09:10,462 --> 00:09:12,464
Those wreck diving ops,
can you do those alone?

209
00:09:12,552 --> 00:09:14,379
No, not if you wanna
live long.

210
00:09:14,466 --> 00:09:17,295
One guy handles the winch,
the other one dives, always.

211
00:09:17,382 --> 00:09:18,993
- Hmm.
- Why?

212
00:09:19,080 --> 00:09:21,256
Lisbon, we should get going.
Uh, Greg.

213
00:09:21,343 --> 00:09:22,866
Real pleasure to meet you.

214
00:09:22,953 --> 00:09:25,260
Thank you very much
for the tea. Take care.

215
00:09:27,828 --> 00:09:29,177
Greg..

216
00:09:30,874 --> 00:09:32,572
...thank you for your time.

217
00:09:43,321 --> 00:09:44,975
Jeeter's really torn up.

218
00:09:45,062 --> 00:09:46,455
So he and Jay got along?

219
00:09:46,542 --> 00:09:49,371
No...jealousy,
arguments over girls?

220
00:09:49,458 --> 00:09:51,068
Best buds.

221
00:09:51,155 --> 00:09:52,809
Jeeter's a good guy.

222
00:09:52,896 --> 00:09:55,072
I'm gonna prorate the rent
till they find another roommate.

223
00:09:55,159 --> 00:09:58,119
- Was Jay the big earner?
- No way.

224
00:09:58,206 --> 00:10:00,861
'I mean, all these guys
are living hand to mouth.'

225
00:10:00,948 --> 00:10:02,689
Jay could hardly buy food.

226
00:10:02,776 --> 00:10:05,648
He was talking about moving out
and living on his boat.

227
00:10:05,735 --> 00:10:07,345
What boat?

228
00:10:07,432 --> 00:10:10,044
Some cheap, old powerboat.

229
00:10:10,131 --> 00:10:11,915
'Jay had a berth somewhere.'

230
00:10:12,002 --> 00:10:14,396
[dramatic music]

231
00:10:17,617 --> 00:10:18,966
Oh, here you go, man.

232
00:10:19,053 --> 00:10:20,184
Thanks.

233
00:10:23,405 --> 00:10:25,799
[music continues]

234
00:10:35,722 --> 00:10:37,985
Coke.

235
00:10:38,072 --> 00:10:39,943
Looks like enough
to sell.

236
00:10:40,030 --> 00:10:41,684
Aw, dude,
that's seriously uncool.

237
00:10:41,771 --> 00:10:45,383
It's a lightning bolt. I've seen
this before in Sacramento.

238
00:10:45,470 --> 00:10:47,429
Jay was in the city last week.

239
00:10:47,516 --> 00:10:49,126
Said he had business.

240
00:10:49,213 --> 00:10:51,302
No sign of a murder weapon
on the beach.

241
00:10:51,389 --> 00:10:52,826
Coroner thinks
it was something heavy.

242
00:10:52,913 --> 00:10:55,263
Possibly metal.

243
00:10:55,350 --> 00:10:58,440
Hey, chief. Any sign
of the victim's powerboat?

244
00:10:58,527 --> 00:11:00,834
Not yet.
It's not at the docks.

245
00:11:00,921 --> 00:11:03,053
Jay lost his berth
for non-payment of rent.

246
00:11:03,140 --> 00:11:05,403
Might have it trailered
somewhere on land.

247
00:11:05,490 --> 00:11:07,405
We're happy to give you
some extra people to search.

248
00:11:07,492 --> 00:11:11,801
If that boat is in Santa Marta,
I'll find it.

249
00:11:11,888 --> 00:11:14,151
No help from CBI is necessary,
but you know what?

250
00:11:15,500 --> 00:11:16,806
Thank you very much.

251
00:11:20,375 --> 00:11:21,811
[female #1]
'Hello?'

252
00:11:21,898 --> 00:11:23,770
Isn't there a coke dealer
in Sacramento..

253
00:11:23,857 --> 00:11:25,597
...uses a lightning bolt
as a logo?

254
00:11:25,685 --> 00:11:27,512
[female #1]
'Well, hello to you, too.'

255
00:11:27,599 --> 00:11:29,514
'Lightning bolt..'

256
00:11:29,601 --> 00:11:31,342
Tookie Burroughs used
to use a lightning bolt.

257
00:11:31,429 --> 00:11:32,953
Maybe he still does.

258
00:11:33,040 --> 00:11:34,345
The victim
had dealer-quantity coke

259
00:11:34,432 --> 00:11:35,999
with that logo on every bag.

260
00:11:36,086 --> 00:11:38,001
Oh, well, you know,
I know Tookie from way back.

261
00:11:38,088 --> 00:11:39,568
- If you want, I could--
- 'No, no.'

262
00:11:39,655 --> 00:11:41,309
Don't even think about it.
I just need a name.

263
00:11:41,396 --> 00:11:42,440
And I'll take it from there.

264
00:11:42,527 --> 00:11:44,312
You're no fun at all.

265
00:11:44,399 --> 00:11:46,706
Our victim was a semi-pro
surfer, name of Jay Banner.

266
00:11:46,793 --> 00:11:48,055
You can ask around about him.

267
00:11:48,142 --> 00:11:49,491
See if he was
a real player or not.

268
00:11:49,578 --> 00:11:51,928
Jay Banner. Okay.

269
00:11:52,015 --> 00:11:53,538
I love you, boo.
Come home soon.

270
00:11:54,801 --> 00:11:55,889
[Cho]
Bye, babe.

271
00:12:08,162 --> 00:12:09,946
Was that Summer?

272
00:12:10,033 --> 00:12:12,470
Yeah.

273
00:12:12,557 --> 00:12:15,517
Sorry to be direct, but what's
going on with you and her?

274
00:12:15,604 --> 00:12:17,649
[sighs]
She's my confidential informant.

275
00:12:17,737 --> 00:12:19,913
Right..

276
00:12:20,000 --> 00:12:22,219
...but we're not allowed
to date CIs.

277
00:12:24,178 --> 00:12:25,745
You're givingme
dating advice?

278
00:12:30,271 --> 00:12:33,883
As a friend,
even if she weren't your CI..

279
00:12:33,970 --> 00:12:35,189
...are you sure
she's someone you should..

280
00:12:36,930 --> 00:12:38,105
...be around?

281
00:12:41,717 --> 00:12:42,762
Let's go.

282
00:12:44,024 --> 00:12:45,416
[engine starts]

283
00:12:47,027 --> 00:12:48,898
[Jane]
'Well, Greg seems very nice.'

284
00:12:48,985 --> 00:12:50,770
I am not discussing it
with you.

285
00:12:50,857 --> 00:12:54,861
Nice, solid, dependable.
I can see why you dumped him.

286
00:12:54,948 --> 00:12:56,645
- I mean it, Jane.
- Alright.

287
00:12:56,732 --> 00:12:57,864
- Seriously.
- Alright.

288
00:13:02,390 --> 00:13:05,828
I didn't dump him.
We both decided to take a break.

289
00:13:05,915 --> 00:13:07,699
Mm-hmm.

290
00:13:07,787 --> 00:13:09,136
The old, let's take a break.

291
00:13:10,354 --> 00:13:11,529
[Lisbon]
I did not dump him.

292
00:13:13,401 --> 00:13:14,576
[Tookie]
'Sixty bucks.'

293
00:13:14,663 --> 00:13:16,752
Sixty? Come on, Tookie.

294
00:13:16,839 --> 00:13:19,102
What do you say 40
for old time's sake?

295
00:13:19,189 --> 00:13:21,844
Sure, after you do me
for old time's sake.

296
00:13:21,931 --> 00:13:23,672
[chuckles]

297
00:13:23,759 --> 00:13:26,196
Is this the same stuff you sell
to my boy, Jay from Santa Marta?

298
00:13:27,458 --> 00:13:29,286
'Cause that-that was good.

299
00:13:31,027 --> 00:13:32,333
- Jay?
- Mmm.

300
00:13:34,944 --> 00:13:35,902
Jay Banner.

301
00:13:35,989 --> 00:13:37,251
[cell phone ringing]

302
00:13:37,338 --> 00:13:38,730
- Oh, yeah. The surf punk.
- Mm-hmm.

303
00:13:38,818 --> 00:13:40,950
I like that dude.
He's hardcore.

304
00:13:41,037 --> 00:13:42,082
Hang on a second.

305
00:13:43,344 --> 00:13:44,780
- Ahem.
- Yeah? So speak.

306
00:13:46,129 --> 00:13:47,696
Oh!

307
00:13:47,783 --> 00:13:49,611
'Yeah, y-you tell him
I told him to shove it down'

308
00:13:49,698 --> 00:13:51,831
'his mother's throat,
the little punk bitch.'

309
00:13:54,877 --> 00:13:56,923
Moron.

310
00:13:57,010 --> 00:13:59,795
So I heard Jay was, uh,
in some beef with some people.

311
00:14:01,188 --> 00:14:02,972
- Oh, yeah? I never heard that.
- No?

312
00:14:11,502 --> 00:14:12,852
It's good stuff.

313
00:14:15,028 --> 00:14:16,203
See you around, Tookie.

314
00:14:17,291 --> 00:14:18,466
Yeah, see you, babes.

315
00:14:31,348 --> 00:14:32,349
Summer, you here?

316
00:14:33,916 --> 00:14:35,483
[water running]

317
00:14:43,839 --> 00:14:44,884
I got Indian.

318
00:14:46,668 --> 00:14:48,626
[Summer]
'Hi, baby, um..'

319
00:14:48,713 --> 00:14:51,368
Hey, I'm gonna hang
with my girlfriends tonight.

320
00:14:51,455 --> 00:14:53,109
'Do you mind? Rain check?'

321
00:14:56,852 --> 00:14:58,593
You come out
and talk to me, okay?

322
00:14:58,680 --> 00:15:00,421
[chuckles]
'Babe, I'm in the shower.'

323
00:15:01,770 --> 00:15:04,033
Something's wrong.

324
00:15:04,120 --> 00:15:06,470
Come on out.
I mean it. Right now.

325
00:15:06,557 --> 00:15:10,126
'Kimball, this is my house,
not yours.'

326
00:15:10,213 --> 00:15:11,998
'I'll come out
when I feel like it.'

327
00:15:12,085 --> 00:15:14,826
- Step away from the door.
- 'No.'

328
00:15:14,914 --> 00:15:16,263
'Kimball, don't.'

329
00:15:16,350 --> 00:15:17,394
Ah!

330
00:15:22,965 --> 00:15:23,966
You never listen.

331
00:15:33,323 --> 00:15:35,238
It was just a mugging.
It's no big deal.

332
00:15:36,761 --> 00:15:38,111
Where?

333
00:15:38,198 --> 00:15:39,764
Downtown.

334
00:15:39,851 --> 00:15:43,072
Some punk kid.
It's just bruises. I'm fine.

335
00:15:45,161 --> 00:15:46,597
- Ow!
- Sorry.

336
00:15:50,688 --> 00:15:51,733
[sighing]

337
00:15:51,820 --> 00:15:53,561
If it was just a mugging..

338
00:15:53,648 --> 00:15:55,041
...why would you not
want me to see you?

339
00:15:56,781 --> 00:15:58,131
Hmm?

340
00:15:58,218 --> 00:15:59,349
Summer, look at me.

341
00:16:00,568 --> 00:16:01,612
Tell me the truth.

342
00:16:03,136 --> 00:16:04,354
Did you go see that dealer?

343
00:16:05,790 --> 00:16:07,792
No.

344
00:16:07,879 --> 00:16:09,098
You went to see
Tookie Burroughs.

345
00:16:09,185 --> 00:16:10,752
You asked him
about Jay Banner.

346
00:16:10,839 --> 00:16:12,493
No, I swear I didn't.

347
00:16:18,368 --> 00:16:19,413
Okay, fine.

348
00:16:21,154 --> 00:16:23,243
It wasn't a mugging.

349
00:16:23,330 --> 00:16:24,722
But..

350
00:16:24,809 --> 00:16:28,422
I ran into some guy
I used to party with and..

351
00:16:28,509 --> 00:16:30,990
...he got mad when I told him I
didn't want to do that anymore.

352
00:16:31,077 --> 00:16:32,600
Happens all the time,
alright?

353
00:16:32,687 --> 00:16:35,516
Can we please
just forget about it?

354
00:16:35,603 --> 00:16:37,083
What was his name?

355
00:16:37,170 --> 00:16:38,258
This guy you used to know.

356
00:16:41,000 --> 00:16:44,742
I don't know his name?
I-I don't, I don't know what..

357
00:16:44,829 --> 00:16:47,267
I remember Bob or..

358
00:16:47,354 --> 00:16:49,182
...Steve.

359
00:16:49,269 --> 00:16:51,488
I don't know, Kimball.
Please.

360
00:16:51,575 --> 00:16:53,925
Can we just forget
about it, please?

361
00:16:56,885 --> 00:16:58,017
Get some rest.

362
00:17:06,938 --> 00:17:08,592
- Morning, boss.
- Morning. How's Ben?

363
00:17:08,679 --> 00:17:11,508
Oh, yeah, he's better, thanks.
It's some kind of ear infection.

364
00:17:11,595 --> 00:17:13,249
Easy to knock out
with antibiotics.

365
00:17:13,336 --> 00:17:15,773
Gave him a fever and made him,
you know, throw up.

366
00:17:15,860 --> 00:17:17,906
- A lot of throw up.
- Aw, poor thing.

367
00:17:17,993 --> 00:17:20,169
Did we have any luck
finding The Hang Ten?

368
00:17:20,256 --> 00:17:21,344
The victim's powerboat?

369
00:17:21,431 --> 00:17:23,085
Oh! No.

370
00:17:23,172 --> 00:17:25,783
Chief Anson asked all over
Santa Marta. Nothing yet.

371
00:17:25,870 --> 00:17:28,177
Uh, what about the mystery
vessel the roommate saw

372
00:17:28,264 --> 00:17:30,223
coming in? We need to talk
to whoever was on that boat.

373
00:17:30,310 --> 00:17:32,964
Yeah, we talked to every
boater in town. Sorry.

374
00:17:33,052 --> 00:17:34,444
Uh, Rigs, you got something
on your..

375
00:17:36,098 --> 00:17:38,361
- Is that spit-up?
- Mmm. Yeah.

376
00:17:38,448 --> 00:17:39,928
It's from this morning.

377
00:17:40,015 --> 00:17:41,147
I was feeding him,
and he was looking up at me

378
00:17:41,234 --> 00:17:43,497
with those big,
adorable eyes.

379
00:17:43,584 --> 00:17:44,672
And then he barfed.

380
00:17:44,759 --> 00:17:46,108
- Kids, huh?
- Yeah.

381
00:17:47,675 --> 00:17:49,720
You know what?

382
00:17:49,807 --> 00:17:52,071
He's the best thing
that ever happened to me.

383
00:17:52,158 --> 00:17:53,768
Wow. That's great.

384
00:18:09,653 --> 00:18:12,352
Morning, chief.
Trapping those tourists early?

385
00:18:12,439 --> 00:18:15,398
Permit-only spot.
Clearly posted.

386
00:18:15,485 --> 00:18:17,487
They can get it out of impound
if they have $200.

387
00:18:17,574 --> 00:18:18,880
'That's the law.'

388
00:18:18,967 --> 00:18:21,361
There's no tyranny
like petty tyranny.

389
00:18:21,448 --> 00:18:24,190
- Excuse me?
- You heard me.

390
00:18:24,277 --> 00:18:25,365
Where you going?

391
00:18:25,452 --> 00:18:26,409
Research library.

392
00:18:27,976 --> 00:18:29,499
That's the bar.

393
00:18:29,586 --> 00:18:30,674
[blows raspberry]

394
00:18:33,982 --> 00:18:35,810
I'm going to meet Jane
in Santa Marta.

395
00:18:35,897 --> 00:18:37,159
Has Cho been in yet?

396
00:18:37,246 --> 00:18:38,856
- I haven't seen him.
- Alright.

397
00:18:38,943 --> 00:18:40,858
Oh, hey, I interviewed
some of Jay's surfing buddies

398
00:18:40,945 --> 00:18:42,295
and we've got a lead, maybe.

399
00:18:42,382 --> 00:18:44,558
- Yeah?
- Possible angry girlfriend.

400
00:18:44,645 --> 00:18:46,386
Day before the murder,
these guys on the beach

401
00:18:46,473 --> 00:18:49,606
saw Jay getting yelled at
on his cell phone by some woman.

402
00:18:49,693 --> 00:18:50,912
Apparently,
it was pretty heated.

403
00:18:50,999 --> 00:18:53,610
Why didn't the roommates
tell us about her?

404
00:18:53,697 --> 00:18:55,177
Maybe they didn't know.

405
00:18:55,264 --> 00:18:57,223
Oh, and bad news
about our drug dealer.

406
00:18:57,310 --> 00:18:59,790
The guy Banner
was getting the coke from?

407
00:18:59,877 --> 00:19:01,662
'Cho got the name.'

408
00:19:01,749 --> 00:19:04,621
Carlton Tookie Burroughs,
but he's got an alibi.

409
00:19:04,708 --> 00:19:06,580
Sac PD arrested him
the night before the murder

410
00:19:06,667 --> 00:19:08,277
and didn't kick him
until noon the next day.

411
00:19:08,364 --> 00:19:09,844
Hours after Banner died.

412
00:19:09,931 --> 00:19:11,889
Alright, strike Burroughs
from the list.

413
00:19:11,976 --> 00:19:13,804
And if anything with
the girlfriend pops, call me.

414
00:19:13,891 --> 00:19:14,892
Alright.

415
00:19:16,155 --> 00:19:18,113
Hey.

416
00:19:18,200 --> 00:19:20,246
Wayne, you've got
something on your..

417
00:19:20,333 --> 00:19:22,030
Oh, yeah. It's just vomit.

418
00:19:24,163 --> 00:19:25,164
Okay.

419
00:19:42,833 --> 00:19:45,836
Uh, Vicki, another round
for my friends here.

420
00:19:45,923 --> 00:19:46,837
You got it, Mr. Jane.

421
00:19:46,924 --> 00:19:48,230
- Jane--
- Shh.

422
00:19:48,317 --> 00:19:49,710
We're getting
at the best part here.

423
00:19:49,797 --> 00:19:51,407
Fierce storm,
low visibility.

424
00:19:51,494 --> 00:19:54,149
A rock has just torn a hole
in the Fremont's hull

425
00:19:54,236 --> 00:19:56,282
as big as a man.
Carry on, Arkin.

426
00:19:56,369 --> 00:19:57,805
Captain's ordered everyone
to abandon ship.

427
00:19:57,892 --> 00:19:58,849
Mm-hmm.

428
00:19:58,936 --> 00:20:00,199
But it was too late.

429
00:20:00,286 --> 00:20:01,852
'The Fremont sank.'

430
00:20:01,939 --> 00:20:03,463
All 200 souls aboard her.

431
00:20:05,291 --> 00:20:07,075
My grandfather said that

432
00:20:07,162 --> 00:20:10,209
even with all the noise
from the storm that night

433
00:20:10,296 --> 00:20:12,080
you could hear 'em
screaming for help.

434
00:20:12,167 --> 00:20:13,473
Hmm.

435
00:20:13,560 --> 00:20:15,475
Some nights,
people say that

436
00:20:15,562 --> 00:20:17,216
you can still hear
their cries on the wind.

437
00:20:18,913 --> 00:20:20,610
And no one
ever found the gold?

438
00:20:20,697 --> 00:20:21,872
What gold?

439
00:20:21,959 --> 00:20:23,918
The Fremont was
a treasure ship

440
00:20:24,005 --> 00:20:26,094
most famous
in Santa Marta history.

441
00:20:26,181 --> 00:20:28,052
Yeah, went down in 1906.

442
00:20:28,139 --> 00:20:29,663
She was carrying
all the annual treaty payments

443
00:20:29,750 --> 00:20:31,360
for the Indian tribes
up north.

444
00:20:31,447 --> 00:20:33,580
Hmm, crates of gold coins
and bullion

445
00:20:33,667 --> 00:20:35,843
sixty million dollars worth,
today's value.

446
00:20:35,930 --> 00:20:37,627
And it was never found?

447
00:20:37,714 --> 00:20:40,108
Nah, the San Francisco
earthquake happened

448
00:20:40,195 --> 00:20:42,676
a few weeks after the wreck.

449
00:20:42,763 --> 00:20:44,852
They think it buried
the ship for good.

450
00:20:44,939 --> 00:20:47,463
Mm, but you kept looking,
didn't you?

451
00:20:47,550 --> 00:20:51,467
[chuckles]
Yeah, I kept looking.

452
00:20:51,554 --> 00:20:52,686
Eh?

453
00:20:55,079 --> 00:20:56,777
Ten dollar, Liberty Head.

454
00:20:56,864 --> 00:20:58,257
- Gold Eagle.
- Oh!

455
00:20:58,344 --> 00:21:00,476
I found that
the summer of '72.

456
00:21:00,563 --> 00:21:02,478
[both chuckling]

457
00:21:02,565 --> 00:21:04,480
Best damn day of my life.

458
00:21:04,567 --> 00:21:05,742
Hell, I thought
I'd found the Fremont.

459
00:21:07,396 --> 00:21:10,443
I dove every day
for the next seven summers.

460
00:21:10,530 --> 00:21:12,140
I never found
another damn thing.

461
00:21:14,185 --> 00:21:15,448
She's down there.

462
00:21:16,623 --> 00:21:18,059
She's there.

463
00:21:18,146 --> 00:21:19,626
That's a great story,
Arkin.

464
00:21:20,844 --> 00:21:22,193
Thank you.

465
00:21:22,281 --> 00:21:24,544
Neo? Two-dog?

466
00:21:24,631 --> 00:21:26,328
I've gotta go.
I'll see you around.

467
00:21:26,415 --> 00:21:27,895
Thanks for the drink.

468
00:21:27,982 --> 00:21:29,853
Do you think Jay found
the Fremont's treasure?

469
00:21:29,940 --> 00:21:33,379
Well, Jayandhis partner.
You don't treasure hunt alone.

470
00:21:33,466 --> 00:21:35,685
He did know the salvage business
from working for Greg.

471
00:21:35,772 --> 00:21:37,687
- And he had a boat.
- Oh, boat, yes.

472
00:21:37,774 --> 00:21:39,036
I'd like to take
a look at that.

473
00:21:39,123 --> 00:21:41,604
It's missing.
Chief Anson's looking for it.

474
00:21:44,172 --> 00:21:45,826
[Jane]
'Uh, speaking of Chief Anson..'

475
00:21:47,741 --> 00:21:48,959
Hey!

476
00:21:49,046 --> 00:21:51,005
Hey, that's my car.

477
00:21:51,092 --> 00:21:53,094
- What?
- My car.

478
00:21:53,181 --> 00:21:55,401
This is? Yours?

479
00:21:55,488 --> 00:21:57,577
Oh. Is it, really?

480
00:21:59,100 --> 00:22:01,058
Agent, I'm so sorry.

481
00:22:01,145 --> 00:22:03,887
Bill, unhook her.
She's law enforcement.

482
00:22:03,974 --> 00:22:07,413
Hey, chief. Any luck with,
uh, Jay's missing boat?

483
00:22:07,500 --> 00:22:10,416
- It's not in Santa Marta.
- How do you know that?

484
00:22:10,503 --> 00:22:12,809
Because my men and I
searched everywhere in town

485
00:22:12,896 --> 00:22:15,551
every square inch
of landandwater.

486
00:22:15,638 --> 00:22:17,074
- Really?
- Mm-hmm.

487
00:22:17,161 --> 00:22:19,250
Most impressive.

488
00:22:19,338 --> 00:22:21,383
Thank you.

489
00:22:21,470 --> 00:22:22,602
Bill!

490
00:22:23,603 --> 00:22:25,169
Belay that order.

491
00:22:25,256 --> 00:22:27,911
We're gonna ride with you.
I love a tow truck.

492
00:22:27,998 --> 00:22:29,565
- We are? Why?
- Lao-Tzu.

493
00:22:29,652 --> 00:22:30,958
Lao-Tzu?

494
00:22:31,045 --> 00:22:32,394
Famous Chinese philosopher.

495
00:22:32,481 --> 00:22:33,700
What has he got
to do with anything?

496
00:22:33,787 --> 00:22:35,397
Ah, your unconscious
sexual bias

497
00:22:35,484 --> 00:22:36,877
at work there, Lisbon.

498
00:22:36,964 --> 00:22:38,226
You assumed he was a he.

499
00:22:38,313 --> 00:22:40,228
- Well, was he?
- He was.

500
00:22:40,315 --> 00:22:42,012
- Not the point. Get in.
- Oh, my God.

501
00:22:42,099 --> 00:22:43,405
Thank you.

502
00:22:45,189 --> 00:22:46,147
Chief.

503
00:22:47,844 --> 00:22:48,802
[engine starts]

504
00:22:48,889 --> 00:22:50,064
Whoo.

505
00:22:56,070 --> 00:22:58,942
[indistinct chattering]

506
00:23:07,168 --> 00:23:08,256
[Cho]
'Hey.'

507
00:23:11,433 --> 00:23:14,697
[intense music]

508
00:23:14,784 --> 00:23:15,959
[engine starts]

509
00:23:16,046 --> 00:23:17,352
[engine revving]

510
00:23:17,439 --> 00:23:19,136
Yo, yo, get off me, man!
What are you, nuts?

511
00:23:19,223 --> 00:23:20,224
[both grunt]

512
00:23:23,489 --> 00:23:25,665
Carlton Tookie Burroughs.
Police.

513
00:23:25,752 --> 00:23:27,449
[crashes]

514
00:23:27,536 --> 00:23:29,756
[grunting]

515
00:23:29,843 --> 00:23:32,585
[panting]
What the hell's up, yo?

516
00:23:32,672 --> 00:23:34,238
Summer Edgecombe.
Tell me what happened.

517
00:23:34,325 --> 00:23:36,676
Oh, hey, man,
that bitch is yours?

518
00:23:36,763 --> 00:23:37,807
[thwacks]

519
00:23:38,982 --> 00:23:40,027
[thud]

520
00:23:41,202 --> 00:23:42,377
Why'd you hurt her, Tookie?

521
00:23:42,464 --> 00:23:44,597
Dude, I swear to God

522
00:23:44,684 --> 00:23:46,860
if I knew that she had
a cop pimping for her

523
00:23:46,947 --> 00:23:48,427
I would have let her
have it free, alright?

524
00:23:48,514 --> 00:23:50,037
What are you talking about?

525
00:23:50,124 --> 00:23:53,997
She stole my product,
man, like, six grams.

526
00:23:54,084 --> 00:23:56,696
She won't give it back.
Like I'm an idiot?

527
00:23:56,783 --> 00:23:58,698
Come on, man.
I'm not gonna let that slide.

528
00:23:58,785 --> 00:24:00,656
I got a reputation, alright?

529
00:24:01,875 --> 00:24:03,050
[thwacks]

530
00:24:05,618 --> 00:24:07,271
Stay away from her.

531
00:24:20,023 --> 00:24:22,286
[Jane]
Well, thank you.

532
00:24:22,373 --> 00:24:25,681
[sighs]
Lao-Tzu said that if
you search everywhere

533
00:24:25,768 --> 00:24:27,727
yet cannot find
what you are seeking

534
00:24:27,814 --> 00:24:30,904
it is because what you seek
is already in your possession.

535
00:24:30,991 --> 00:24:32,166
Deep.

536
00:24:32,253 --> 00:24:34,124
What kind of boat
did Jay Banner own?

537
00:24:34,211 --> 00:24:35,386
A gray lapstreak.

538
00:24:35,474 --> 00:24:37,301
Hmm, well, Jay
was a smart man

539
00:24:37,388 --> 00:24:39,303
who needed to park his
boat somewhere for free

540
00:24:39,390 --> 00:24:41,784
so why not put someone
else's plates on the trailer

541
00:24:41,871 --> 00:24:45,309
andletit get towed
by the very eager police?

542
00:24:45,396 --> 00:24:46,746
'Where better
to stash your boat'

543
00:24:46,833 --> 00:24:47,921
'than the one place you know'

544
00:24:48,008 --> 00:24:49,879
the policearen'tgonna look?

545
00:24:49,966 --> 00:24:52,055
And then Jay could
slip in through that gate

546
00:24:52,142 --> 00:24:54,449
'anytime he pleased.'

547
00:24:54,536 --> 00:24:56,538
Ah! There it is.
The Hang Ten.

548
00:24:56,625 --> 00:24:57,887
Bingo, and I'm willing to bet

549
00:24:57,974 --> 00:24:59,759
that there is evidence
of Jay's treasure hunt

550
00:24:59,846 --> 00:25:02,022
maybe even the treasure
itself, on board.

551
00:25:03,937 --> 00:25:04,894
[clangs]

552
00:25:04,981 --> 00:25:05,939
Or the killer.

553
00:25:06,026 --> 00:25:08,942
[intense music]

554
00:25:09,029 --> 00:25:12,032
Police! Come out with
your hands in the air!

555
00:25:14,208 --> 00:25:15,731
- Don't shoot.
- 'Greg?'

556
00:25:16,471 --> 00:25:17,820
Sound advice.

557
00:25:26,568 --> 00:25:28,570
You know what
Jane's theory is?

558
00:25:28,657 --> 00:25:31,138
If Jay really did salvage
the Fremont's treasure

559
00:25:31,225 --> 00:25:34,489
he had to have had a partner.
He couldn't have done it alone.

560
00:25:34,576 --> 00:25:36,535
A partner who killed him.

561
00:25:38,232 --> 00:25:40,756
What were you doing
on Jay's powerboat?

562
00:25:40,843 --> 00:25:43,019
He borrowed a life jacket.
I wanted it back.

563
00:25:43,106 --> 00:25:44,847
How did you even know
where to find the boat?

564
00:25:44,934 --> 00:25:46,719
Jay told me.

565
00:25:46,806 --> 00:25:47,981
Greg, stop it. Alright?

566
00:25:48,068 --> 00:25:49,156
Since I'm 17 years old

567
00:25:49,243 --> 00:25:50,374
I can tell when you're lying.

568
00:25:50,461 --> 00:25:51,506
That's funny,
'cause I could never

569
00:25:51,593 --> 00:25:52,812
tell when you
were lying to me.

570
00:25:52,899 --> 00:25:54,727
[door opens]

571
00:25:54,814 --> 00:25:56,642
- Oh. Hey, boss.
- I got this. Thanks.

572
00:25:58,774 --> 00:26:00,907
- 'I thought I was question--'
- Get outta here.

573
00:26:00,994 --> 00:26:02,125
Okay.

574
00:26:04,780 --> 00:26:06,086
See, I know you're lying

575
00:26:06,173 --> 00:26:08,262
because we found
the cleaning equipment.

576
00:26:08,349 --> 00:26:09,698
You were there
to wipe the place down

577
00:26:09,785 --> 00:26:11,047
a good way
to cover your tracks.

578
00:26:11,134 --> 00:26:13,049
Look, I didn't kill him.

579
00:26:13,136 --> 00:26:14,703
The morning of his
death, Jay's roommate

580
00:26:14,790 --> 00:26:16,879
saw a boat
heading toward shore.

581
00:26:16,966 --> 00:26:18,664
That was you. It was your boat.

582
00:26:18,751 --> 00:26:20,709
Look, sometimes
stuff looks bad

583
00:26:20,796 --> 00:26:22,537
and there's
an innocent explanation.

584
00:26:22,624 --> 00:26:24,234
- Okay, what's the explanation?
- You won't believe me.

585
00:26:24,321 --> 00:26:25,758
Try me.

586
00:26:27,150 --> 00:26:29,500
You know..

587
00:26:29,588 --> 00:26:33,592
...it took a long time for
me to get over you, Teresa.

588
00:26:33,679 --> 00:26:36,682
A long time...
but I finally did.

589
00:26:38,945 --> 00:26:41,730
It was your
boat that morning.

590
00:26:41,817 --> 00:26:43,123
Tell me the truth.

591
00:26:43,210 --> 00:26:44,472
Yes, it was my boat. Alright?

592
00:26:44,559 --> 00:26:45,691
I was out fishing.

593
00:26:45,778 --> 00:26:48,781
No luck.
I didn't catch anything.

594
00:26:48,868 --> 00:26:50,870
Talk to me. I can help you.

595
00:26:50,957 --> 00:26:52,828
No, thanks. I'm good.

596
00:26:56,310 --> 00:26:57,441
[bell rings]

597
00:27:02,272 --> 00:27:03,230
- Arkin.
- Mm.

598
00:27:03,317 --> 00:27:04,274
I'd like to ask you a few more

599
00:27:04,361 --> 00:27:05,885
questions about the Fremont.

600
00:27:05,972 --> 00:27:08,322
Then I've got some answers.

601
00:27:08,409 --> 00:27:10,759
"The Fremont?"
Is this about Jay's murder?

602
00:27:10,846 --> 00:27:14,067
Uh, yeah. In fact, there's about
to be a major break in the case.

603
00:27:15,546 --> 00:27:17,244
In that case,
it's on the house.

604
00:27:17,331 --> 00:27:19,159
Oh, thank you.

605
00:27:19,246 --> 00:27:21,117
Oop, it's my phone.

606
00:27:23,511 --> 00:27:25,687
I'm gonna have to take this.
I'll be right back.

607
00:27:29,822 --> 00:27:31,084
[Rigsby]
'Inventory from
the victim's boat.'

608
00:27:31,171 --> 00:27:32,738
- Thanks. We get any prints?
- No.

609
00:27:32,825 --> 00:27:35,001
Tayback must've wiped 'em all.

610
00:27:35,088 --> 00:27:36,306
Hang on.

611
00:27:36,393 --> 00:27:38,308
"Hydraulic pump,
underwater cutting tools."

612
00:27:38,395 --> 00:27:39,658
Those are all salvage
equipment, right?

613
00:27:39,745 --> 00:27:40,833
Yeah, there was a bunch of 'em.

614
00:27:40,920 --> 00:27:42,051
'Expensive stuff.'

615
00:27:42,138 --> 00:27:43,313
Thanks.

616
00:27:46,360 --> 00:27:47,883
No problem.

617
00:27:53,584 --> 00:27:55,674
[indistinct chattering]

618
00:27:57,023 --> 00:27:58,764
Is any of your
equipment missing?

619
00:27:58,851 --> 00:28:00,679
Go away. Greg said
not to speak with you.

620
00:28:00,766 --> 00:28:03,246
Your salvage equipment,
we found some on Jay's boat.

621
00:28:03,333 --> 00:28:06,206
- So?
- So is any of it yours?

622
00:28:06,293 --> 00:28:08,861
If Greg was Jay's partner
in the salvage of the Fremont

623
00:28:08,948 --> 00:28:10,689
they would have used
your company's equipment.

624
00:28:10,776 --> 00:28:13,300
- Greg didn't do this.
- So prove it.

625
00:28:13,387 --> 00:28:14,736
Is any of your gear missing?

626
00:28:14,823 --> 00:28:16,477
No, it's all accounted for.

627
00:28:16,564 --> 00:28:18,218
So Jay used his own gear

628
00:28:18,305 --> 00:28:20,176
which means Greg
wasn't the partner.

629
00:28:20,263 --> 00:28:22,178
Oh, the killer, you mean?

630
00:28:22,265 --> 00:28:25,486
No, of course not.
Greg couldn't hurt anyone.

631
00:28:25,573 --> 00:28:27,967
He incriminated himself.

632
00:28:28,054 --> 00:28:30,665
Greg told me that he was
on a boat going to see Jay

633
00:28:30,752 --> 00:28:32,667
the morning of his death.

634
00:28:34,451 --> 00:28:37,280
I think he was lying
to protect someone else.

635
00:28:37,367 --> 00:28:39,108
[instrumental music]

636
00:28:39,195 --> 00:28:40,414
Yeah.

637
00:28:40,501 --> 00:28:42,155
You.

638
00:28:42,242 --> 00:28:44,853
It was you on that boat.

639
00:28:44,940 --> 00:28:46,768
Greg made me
promise not to tell.

640
00:28:48,248 --> 00:28:50,990
For our girls, he said.

641
00:28:51,077 --> 00:28:53,166
You were having
an affair with Jay.

642
00:28:53,253 --> 00:28:54,515
[sighs]

643
00:28:56,038 --> 00:28:58,562
I love Greg.

644
00:28:58,649 --> 00:29:01,827
But we hit
a rough patch and..

645
00:29:01,914 --> 00:29:04,743
Now with three kids
and the business and..

646
00:29:06,005 --> 00:29:08,834
Well, Jay was just fun and..

647
00:29:08,921 --> 00:29:11,401
No responsibilities.
It was just fun and..

648
00:29:14,230 --> 00:29:17,538
I wanted to imagine
a different life..

649
00:29:17,625 --> 00:29:20,410
...just for a while.

650
00:29:20,497 --> 00:29:22,369
I mean, haven't you
ever wanted to do that?

651
00:29:27,243 --> 00:29:29,115
Tell me what
happened that morning.

652
00:29:30,681 --> 00:29:33,989
I knew I had to
break it off with Jay.

653
00:29:34,076 --> 00:29:35,904
So I took the boat out
in the morning to end it.

654
00:29:37,950 --> 00:29:41,257
And then I came back and I
confessed everything to Greg.

655
00:29:41,344 --> 00:29:43,259
And I begged
for his forgiveness

656
00:29:43,346 --> 00:29:44,957
and he gave it to me.
God bless him.

657
00:29:45,044 --> 00:29:46,959
Did you kill Jay Banner?

658
00:29:47,046 --> 00:29:49,178
No!

659
00:29:49,265 --> 00:29:51,790
No, he was alive
when I left.

660
00:29:51,877 --> 00:29:54,357
But when Greg and I found
out that he'd been killed

661
00:29:54,444 --> 00:29:56,316
he said we had
to keep it a secret.

662
00:29:58,187 --> 00:30:00,407
He wanted to protect me.

663
00:30:00,494 --> 00:30:03,062
That gives Greg strong
motive to kill him.

664
00:30:03,149 --> 00:30:04,759
Well, he couldn't have..

665
00:30:04,846 --> 00:30:06,805
...because he was
at home with the girls.

666
00:30:07,936 --> 00:30:10,721
And besides, you know him.

667
00:30:10,809 --> 00:30:12,898
You really think
he could kill someone?

668
00:30:13,942 --> 00:30:15,378
[cell phone ringing]

669
00:30:15,465 --> 00:30:16,858
Excuse me.

670
00:30:18,251 --> 00:30:19,469
Jane, what's up?

671
00:30:19,556 --> 00:30:20,514
[Jane on phone]
'Lisbon, get over'

672
00:30:20,601 --> 00:30:21,558
'to Jay Banner's house'

673
00:30:21,645 --> 00:30:22,951
'as fast as possible.'

674
00:30:23,038 --> 00:30:24,997
There's excitement afoot.

675
00:30:25,911 --> 00:30:27,477
'Good.'

676
00:30:27,564 --> 00:30:29,523
'Well, she's on her way.'

677
00:30:31,220 --> 00:30:33,222
What the hell's
going on here?

678
00:30:33,309 --> 00:30:35,877
Yeah, I mean, no offense,
but...what he said.

679
00:30:37,705 --> 00:30:39,620
Well, chief, we need to execute

680
00:30:39,707 --> 00:30:43,015
a thorough and exhaustive
search of these premises.

681
00:30:43,102 --> 00:30:44,973
And what are we looking for?

682
00:30:47,062 --> 00:30:48,237
Treasure.

683
00:30:48,324 --> 00:30:50,849
[birds squawking]

684
00:30:54,026 --> 00:30:56,115
♪ Cases of Bacardi chasers ♪

685
00:30:56,202 --> 00:30:58,378
♪ Chasing me all over town ♪

686
00:30:58,465 --> 00:30:59,901
♪ 'Cause he knows I'm wasted ♪

687
00:30:59,988 --> 00:31:02,556
♪ Facing time again
on Rikers Island ♪

688
00:31:02,643 --> 00:31:05,341
♪ And I won't get out ♪

689
00:31:05,428 --> 00:31:06,690
[Cho]
'Hey.'

690
00:31:06,777 --> 00:31:08,301
Hey!

691
00:31:08,388 --> 00:31:09,868
What?

692
00:31:09,955 --> 00:31:11,086
Hi.

693
00:31:12,609 --> 00:31:14,220
Where is it?

694
00:31:15,438 --> 00:31:17,484
Where's what?

695
00:31:17,571 --> 00:31:20,530
The cocaine you
stole from Burroughs.

696
00:31:20,617 --> 00:31:21,792
He said that?

697
00:31:21,880 --> 00:31:22,837
[gasps]

698
00:31:22,924 --> 00:31:25,057
He's a liar.

699
00:31:25,144 --> 00:31:27,320
I didn't steal any cocaine.
I swear to God, Cho.

700
00:31:27,407 --> 00:31:28,495
I would never steal co--

701
00:31:28,582 --> 00:31:30,540
Summer, don't do this.

702
00:31:30,627 --> 00:31:31,715
Uh.

703
00:31:31,802 --> 00:31:32,934
[chuckles]

704
00:31:33,021 --> 00:31:34,457
[whispers]
Okay, fine.

705
00:31:36,416 --> 00:31:38,548
[sighs]
I lied.

706
00:31:41,464 --> 00:31:43,292
But you got to admit,
I kinda fooled you.

707
00:31:43,379 --> 00:31:45,033
I had you
a little bit fooled.

708
00:31:45,120 --> 00:31:46,817
Give it to me.

709
00:31:46,905 --> 00:31:49,298
No. It's no big deal.

710
00:31:49,385 --> 00:31:51,387
Tookie just thinks
he's dangerous.

711
00:31:51,474 --> 00:31:54,477
I've been beaten far
worse for a lot less.

712
00:31:54,564 --> 00:31:56,392
[shouting]
Give it to me!

713
00:31:57,567 --> 00:31:59,613
Fine. Be that way.

714
00:32:03,269 --> 00:32:05,662
Look, I'm sorry.
You know, I-I-I..

715
00:32:05,749 --> 00:32:08,578
I know I've been bad.
It's just..

716
00:32:08,665 --> 00:32:10,363
I've been so bored.

717
00:32:10,450 --> 00:32:13,061
And this was such a rush.

718
00:32:13,148 --> 00:32:15,237
Summer, I had
to stop myself

719
00:32:15,324 --> 00:32:17,022
from hurting
him badly.

720
00:32:17,109 --> 00:32:20,329
I could have killed him.
You understand me?

721
00:32:20,416 --> 00:32:22,984
God...that's kind of hot.

722
00:32:23,071 --> 00:32:24,855
What's the hell
is wrong with you?

723
00:32:24,943 --> 00:32:26,814
You're really messed up,
you know that?

724
00:32:28,207 --> 00:32:30,949
Well, duh!
Like you're not?

725
00:32:32,689 --> 00:32:34,126
No! What are you doing?

726
00:32:34,213 --> 00:32:36,606
Stop it! Are you nuts?
It was mine!

727
00:32:36,693 --> 00:32:38,695
I worked for it, Cho!

728
00:32:38,782 --> 00:32:40,523
I'm a cop! How did you
think this was gonna go?

729
00:32:40,610 --> 00:32:41,568
[crying]

730
00:32:41,655 --> 00:32:42,656
[clatters]

731
00:32:45,093 --> 00:32:46,747
[crying]

732
00:32:46,834 --> 00:32:49,663
[instrumental music]

733
00:33:11,380 --> 00:33:13,513
[indistinct chattering on radio]

734
00:33:13,600 --> 00:33:14,905
Kitchen and den are clear.

735
00:33:14,993 --> 00:33:16,385
Copy that.

736
00:33:18,518 --> 00:33:21,173
- So is the attic.
- Roger.

737
00:33:21,260 --> 00:33:23,349
- Jane, what's going on?
- Treasure hunt.

738
00:33:23,436 --> 00:33:26,308
- Here?
- Gold's heavy.

739
00:33:26,395 --> 00:33:28,702
And it's hard to transport.
Difficult to store.

740
00:33:28,789 --> 00:33:30,747
They had to hide
the treasure someplace.

741
00:33:30,834 --> 00:33:33,054
Probably near wherever they
brought it into shore.

742
00:33:33,141 --> 00:33:34,795
To keep it safe before
they could sell the gold

743
00:33:34,882 --> 00:33:36,101
but why look here?

744
00:33:36,188 --> 00:33:39,713
Uh, Jeeter?
You were Jay's best friend.

745
00:33:39,800 --> 00:33:43,412
Jay was tricky, sly, always
a couple of moves ahead, no?

746
00:33:43,499 --> 00:33:44,544
Yeah.

747
00:33:44,631 --> 00:33:46,546
He was an operator, sure.

748
00:33:46,633 --> 00:33:47,982
He was an operator.

749
00:33:48,069 --> 00:33:49,810
Jay's partner
double-crossed Jay

750
00:33:49,897 --> 00:33:51,681
killed him,
but what he didn't know

751
00:33:51,768 --> 00:33:53,770
is that Jay
double-crossed him first.

752
00:33:53,857 --> 00:33:56,817
Jay moved the treasure
before he died.

753
00:33:56,904 --> 00:33:58,297
- Seriously?
- Yeah.

754
00:33:58,384 --> 00:34:00,429
And I'm guessing
he moved it here.

755
00:34:00,516 --> 00:34:01,778
I got something.

756
00:34:01,865 --> 00:34:02,823
I found this in the trunk

757
00:34:02,910 --> 00:34:04,172
of that Toyota out in the drive.

758
00:34:04,259 --> 00:34:06,131
Oh, yeah? What is it?

759
00:34:08,176 --> 00:34:09,308
Oh.

760
00:34:10,439 --> 00:34:11,571
[chuckles]

761
00:34:11,658 --> 00:34:12,746
'Treasure.'

762
00:34:19,231 --> 00:34:21,407
You found this
here, in the trunk?

763
00:34:21,494 --> 00:34:23,322
- Yep.
- This is amazing.

764
00:34:23,409 --> 00:34:25,541
No. No, it's awful.

765
00:34:25,628 --> 00:34:26,890
Why?

766
00:34:26,977 --> 00:34:30,416
Because the gold's gone,
maybe forever.

767
00:34:30,503 --> 00:34:31,591
I was slightly off.

768
00:34:31,678 --> 00:34:32,853
When Jay double-crossed
his partner

769
00:34:32,940 --> 00:34:34,115
he-he didn't bring
the gold here.

770
00:34:34,202 --> 00:34:35,551
He used this car
to take the gold

771
00:34:35,638 --> 00:34:36,987
somewhere else entirely.

772
00:34:37,075 --> 00:34:38,946
Or maybe the killer did.

773
00:34:41,340 --> 00:34:43,211
- Whose car is this?
- Mine.

774
00:34:45,605 --> 00:34:46,910
Jay had a key too.

775
00:34:46,997 --> 00:34:48,999
He must have borrowed it.

776
00:34:49,087 --> 00:34:50,827
Or maybe you
moved the gold

777
00:34:50,914 --> 00:34:52,264
after you killed Jay.

778
00:34:52,351 --> 00:34:54,353
- You, screw you, Tark!
- 'No, chill!'

779
00:34:54,440 --> 00:34:58,183
Chill, chill! This isn't
helping. So what now?

780
00:34:58,270 --> 00:35:00,489
Well, now we go home, watch TV.

781
00:35:00,576 --> 00:35:02,665
Rent "Treasure Island"
if it's not too depressing.

782
00:35:07,061 --> 00:35:08,715
Alright, what's
really going on?

783
00:35:08,802 --> 00:35:11,021
Cho and Van Pelt checked
that trunk. There was no coin.

784
00:35:11,109 --> 00:35:12,762
I've baited a trap, Lisbon.

785
00:35:12,849 --> 00:35:15,200
The killer will be compelled
to check on the treasure.

786
00:35:15,287 --> 00:35:17,811
"Bait." The coin. It was
Mr. Shea's, wasn't it?

787
00:35:17,898 --> 00:35:20,509
Well, he's not gonna mind.
It makes for a good story.

788
00:35:20,596 --> 00:35:22,468
His coin will catch a killer.

789
00:35:26,776 --> 00:35:28,300
[car doors close]

790
00:35:28,387 --> 00:35:30,737
Jay never moved
the gold, did he?

791
00:35:30,824 --> 00:35:33,870
Nope, it's still exactly
where he and the killer hid it.

792
00:35:33,957 --> 00:35:36,003
Only now the killer
is gonna feel compelled

793
00:35:36,090 --> 00:35:38,788
to check and see if
it really has moved.

794
00:35:38,875 --> 00:35:40,268
[engine starts]

795
00:35:53,020 --> 00:35:54,413
- So..
- Ahem.

796
00:35:54,500 --> 00:35:56,154
Why didn't you let me in
on your brilliant plan?

797
00:35:56,241 --> 00:35:58,243
Oh, you were very
busy with Greg.

798
00:35:58,330 --> 00:36:00,941
I'm never too busy
to solve a murder.

799
00:36:01,028 --> 00:36:04,292
Well, you seemed to be
very busywith Greg.

800
00:36:04,379 --> 00:36:06,381
Oh, hush.

801
00:36:06,468 --> 00:36:08,470
Alright, I'm hushing.

802
00:36:08,557 --> 00:36:09,689
[Jane]
'Look.'

803
00:36:09,776 --> 00:36:12,692
[intense music]

804
00:36:28,838 --> 00:36:30,318
[Jane]
Ahoy there!

805
00:36:30,405 --> 00:36:33,147
[Lisbon]
Put your hands in the air!

806
00:36:33,234 --> 00:36:34,192
[Jane]
'Ah.'

807
00:36:35,454 --> 00:36:36,716
Gladstone.

808
00:36:38,065 --> 00:36:39,849
And what do we have here?

809
00:36:41,547 --> 00:36:42,809
[latches click]

810
00:36:46,682 --> 00:36:50,033
Aye. That be the treasure.

811
00:36:54,168 --> 00:36:55,387
It was you that
discovered the location

812
00:36:55,474 --> 00:36:57,084
of the Fremont, right?

813
00:36:58,738 --> 00:36:59,695
Yeah.

814
00:37:01,219 --> 00:37:02,263
[Gladstone]
We had a big storm

815
00:37:02,350 --> 00:37:03,308
a month back.

816
00:37:03,395 --> 00:37:04,439
Shifted everything around.

817
00:37:04,526 --> 00:37:05,484
I could see the wreck

818
00:37:05,571 --> 00:37:06,876
on sonar clear as clear.

819
00:37:06,963 --> 00:37:08,356
[Van Pelt]
But you have asthma.

820
00:37:08,443 --> 00:37:09,923
You couldn't dive
for it yourself.

821
00:37:11,794 --> 00:37:14,710
Jay knew salvage...and I
knew he needed the bread bad

822
00:37:14,797 --> 00:37:17,147
so...we partnered up.

823
00:37:17,235 --> 00:37:18,845
And the morning he died?

824
00:37:21,848 --> 00:37:23,719
[Gladstone]
I saw Jay out on the beach.

825
00:37:25,504 --> 00:37:27,332
Thought I'd talk
to him, that's all.

826
00:37:28,855 --> 00:37:30,726
You know,
one partner to another.

827
00:37:33,425 --> 00:37:35,209
I had the hammer
on my belt, you know?

828
00:37:35,296 --> 00:37:36,428
[thud]

829
00:37:37,820 --> 00:37:40,040
I'm a peaceful man.

830
00:37:40,127 --> 00:37:42,347
I can't explain it.

831
00:37:42,434 --> 00:37:44,087
[Van Pelt]
You didn't wanna
share your treasure.

832
00:37:50,442 --> 00:37:53,183
He wanted half,
half of what I found.

833
00:37:54,489 --> 00:37:55,795
'I mean, is that right?'

834
00:37:58,798 --> 00:38:01,191
I don't know
what came over me.

835
00:38:01,279 --> 00:38:03,193
It's called greed.

836
00:38:06,632 --> 00:38:08,982
[birds squawking]

837
00:38:09,069 --> 00:38:11,027
[footsteps approaching]

838
00:38:11,114 --> 00:38:12,115
[Lisbon]
Hey.

839
00:38:13,465 --> 00:38:16,163
Oh, hey.

840
00:38:16,250 --> 00:38:18,687
I cleared it with Chief Anson.

841
00:38:18,774 --> 00:38:22,300
Nobody's gonna prosecute you
for breaking into Jay's boat.

842
00:38:22,387 --> 00:38:24,258
Well, thank you.

843
00:38:24,345 --> 00:38:27,870
You were there to wipe your
wife's prints, weren't you?

844
00:38:27,957 --> 00:38:31,961
Yeah, they met there
a couple of times, her and Jay.

845
00:38:32,048 --> 00:38:33,746
And I knew if you found
out about the affair

846
00:38:33,833 --> 00:38:35,443
you'd get the wrong idea.

847
00:38:35,530 --> 00:38:38,054
So you incriminated yourself.

848
00:38:38,141 --> 00:38:41,536
Hey, she's my wife.
I love her.

849
00:38:41,623 --> 00:38:45,018
I mean, it's gonna take a lot
of work, but I won't give up.

850
00:38:45,105 --> 00:38:47,673
You never did
give up on people.

851
00:38:47,760 --> 00:38:51,241
You're a good man.
She's lucky to have you.

852
00:38:51,329 --> 00:38:52,504
You're strong now,
you know that?

853
00:38:52,591 --> 00:38:55,202
You didn't used
to be so strong.

854
00:38:55,289 --> 00:38:57,509
I bet you're really
good at your job.

855
00:38:57,596 --> 00:39:00,816
Yeah. Yeah,
I'm okay, I think.

856
00:39:00,903 --> 00:39:03,558
You seem happy.

857
00:39:03,645 --> 00:39:05,212
I am.

858
00:39:05,299 --> 00:39:06,909
Yeah. I am.

859
00:39:06,996 --> 00:39:09,129
I'm glad we met again.

860
00:39:09,216 --> 00:39:11,436
You and me, you know?

861
00:39:13,786 --> 00:39:16,484
I'm sorry for running
off the way I did.

862
00:39:16,571 --> 00:39:18,660
Hey, it's no biggie.

863
00:39:19,618 --> 00:39:21,489
Forget it.

864
00:39:21,576 --> 00:39:24,449
[instrumental music]

865
00:39:27,408 --> 00:39:29,323
Take care of yourself.

866
00:39:30,933 --> 00:39:33,719
[music continues]

867
00:39:44,077 --> 00:39:45,774
Was Arkin mad
about the coin?

868
00:39:45,861 --> 00:39:48,951
No, like I said, it's another
story for him to tell.

869
00:39:49,038 --> 00:39:50,953
Always good to
have another story.

870
00:39:52,738 --> 00:39:54,653
Don't be wistful, Lisbon.

871
00:39:54,740 --> 00:39:56,306
Greg was never
the right man for you.

872
00:39:56,394 --> 00:39:59,484
I'm not wistful,
and, no, he wasn't.

873
00:39:59,571 --> 00:40:02,008
Far too intense and particular
for a man like that.

874
00:40:02,095 --> 00:40:04,445
Excuse me?
"Intense and particular?"

875
00:40:04,532 --> 00:40:06,491
- What is that supposed to mean?
- Well, they're good things.

876
00:40:06,578 --> 00:40:08,841
In what ways am I
"intense and particular?"

877
00:40:08,928 --> 00:40:11,017
- Oh, please.
- Tell me.

878
00:40:11,104 --> 00:40:14,063
Well, what ever happened
to that guy from narcotics?

879
00:40:14,150 --> 00:40:15,108
- Jeff?
- Jeff.

880
00:40:15,195 --> 00:40:16,370
Oh, he bit his nails.

881
00:40:16,457 --> 00:40:17,415
See, that's particular.

882
00:40:17,502 --> 00:40:18,677
That is not being particular.

883
00:40:18,764 --> 00:40:20,113
It's a disgusting habit.

884
00:40:20,200 --> 00:40:21,636
[Jane]
'There's intense.'

885
00:40:29,514 --> 00:40:31,385
Is your sister gonna
meet you at the station?

886
00:40:31,472 --> 00:40:35,258
Oh, yeah. She can't wait
to get me in her clutches again.

887
00:40:35,345 --> 00:40:37,435
- Okay, good.
- Yeah.

888
00:40:37,522 --> 00:40:39,306
Got, uh, spaghetti
and meatballs

889
00:40:39,393 --> 00:40:41,700
with the nephews,
and, um

890
00:40:41,787 --> 00:40:44,659
Seattle Community College,
here I come.

891
00:40:44,746 --> 00:40:46,661
Whoo-hoo.

892
00:40:46,748 --> 00:40:48,707
[instrumental music]

893
00:40:48,794 --> 00:40:50,665
This is the right thing.

894
00:40:52,798 --> 00:40:54,756
But I don't
wanna say goodbye.

895
00:40:56,802 --> 00:41:00,240
Maybe you could, uh,
come visit me sometime.

896
00:41:02,285 --> 00:41:04,113
Yeah. Maybe.

897
00:41:04,200 --> 00:41:05,985
[man on PA]
'Last call for
the Coast Moonlight'

898
00:41:06,072 --> 00:41:10,206
'train number 7-14
to Seattle, track 2.'

899
00:41:10,293 --> 00:41:12,992
'Once again, last call
for the Coast Moonlight'

900
00:41:13,079 --> 00:41:15,603
'train number 7-14
to Seattle, track 2.'

901
00:41:15,690 --> 00:41:18,214
"Coast Moonlight."
That sounds romantic.

902
00:41:20,216 --> 00:41:21,870
You better go.

903
00:41:21,957 --> 00:41:24,830
[instrumental music]

904
00:41:33,316 --> 00:41:35,231
Goodbye, Kimball.

905
00:41:35,318 --> 00:41:38,234
[music continues]

906
00:41:51,813 --> 00:41:54,642
[music continues]

907
00:42:03,521 --> 00:42:04,696
Bye.

908
00:42:04,783 --> 00:42:07,481
[music continues]

909
00:42:18,405 --> 00:42:20,146
[theme music]

